a San Diego the home of snowboarding  legends Shaun White and psionics the  makers of rocket League psionics is  currently testing the Xbox version of  the game set to release in about a month  other than that they're making more art  assets to bring players new cars  antennas and some secret stuff they  wouldn't talk about just yet  I'm here to see what they've been up to  lately you might not know this but  bionics makes more than just rocket  League this studio has been turning out  games since 2008 the year they first  released the original supersonic  acrobatic rocket powered battle cars  over the next seven years they would  grow to over 50 people strong and worked  tirelessly to find a better name for the  same game the success of rocket League  was a surprise to everyone so now they  work to add new content to the game and  come up with new ways to get players  involved so I sat down with design  director Corey Davis to talk at length  about the games release and their new  focus on community maps I would say up  until the day it came out we weren't  really sure how well it would do we  still kind of had an itching feeling on  back of our heads that it was like a  niche game and no one would really like  it was too hard so it was really like  sick flabbergasting the bleach that it  had and I think that PlayStation Plus  still really got it into a lot of  people's hands that might not have tried  it but that was really awesome just we  were just blown away depending on how  well the game did we didn't know if we  could make much DLC if we could afford  to so it's really allowed us to kind of  stretch our legs a little bit and see  like what what do we want to do for  rocket League for the release of rocket  League we really wanted to focus on like  a very good standard arena and build  around that and some of our original  fans were like you know what do you guys  do with the crazy maps from the first  game and frankly the the real limitation  is it just takes us a lot of time to  make a map to the art quality that you  see in the game rocket labs was just an  internal sort of like pet project of a  couple of us where we said what if we  made a not holodeck but kind of like  bare-bones tile set that we could just  release a bunch of maps on more quickly  and see what the community  we're gonna have three maps to start  with for the first release they're all  just completely different types of maps  than what you've seen before so there's  a throwback to the first game it's a  circular sort of donut map we're calling  a retro utopia and it's got you can  actually drive between the goals in a  tunnel and then the other two are more  focused on different ways to play rocket  League so one has very raised sides to  the map so there's a lot more  verticality than you only see and the  other one actually has a split so  there's two gold entrances and we find  that really changed up the gameplay too  we had some of our pro players saying  they wish there was more height variants  because they wanted to see what kind of  interesting aerial play you would get  when it wasn't just everyone leaping  from flat ground and that one even on  our limited testing is seen really cool  sort of like people diving down onto the  ball from above rather than just flying  up to but everything we're doing is sort  of like step by step like let's let you  guys try this out and then if everyone  likes it or if the most of you like it  then we'll push it into rank but we're  being very careful about tampering with  what we feel is sort of like the very  pure core essence of rocket League so  let's take a better look at these maps  and what you can expect first up is  utopia retro which is a throwback to the  original game but with Tron skin you can  drive through the goal to get on the  offensive or defense must also a ramp in  the middle of the doughnut it's got a  boost in it so it feels really good to  hit it in the middle of a battle  underpass is two-tiered with the second  level on the outer rim that ramps over  the goal resulting in more teamwork  based aerial hits in fact the whole  level lends itself more to high-flying  action to keep up with the elevated  sides  finally double goal is what you expected  it's a double goal with a net in between  this one is my least favorite of the  three because the extra lip in between  the goals increases your chances of your  car getting out of control you can drive  through these goals as well which will  make for some interesting team plays all  three of the maps encourage more  communication and new play tactics  you'll have to adapt your skills to meet  the needs of each map and although these  maps aren't currently in ranked  playlists they have the potential to be  added rocket labs might be a breath of  fresh air for players looking to mix up  traditional gameplay but not everyone is  interested in mutaters snowed  and my least-favorite wasteland and with  all the various match options they're  dividing their player base which can  make it harder to be matched against  someone with a similar skill level so at  the end of the day what is the core  gameplay psyonix wants their players to  experience and how does rocket labs add  to that when you play a match you win on  your skill alone and your teams go on  and not like dice rolls or you know it's  not like a MOBA where you pick your hero  poorly like it's every match it's just  sheerly how well you can manipulate your  car and your team's cars so I think  that's why we think there's room for  maps like this because it's like soccer  wouldn't change the layout of the field  sometimes the argument we see against  changing up the core playing field but  when you look at rocket League more as a  physics sport and reacting to how the  ball interacts with your environment  than we think there is the potentially  room there so that's something we want  to explore with the community you can  expect to play rocket labs in the next  February update and whether or not these  maps are up your alley at least psionics  is listening
 

